# 2026-08-19, No.471 -- 55 -> 54. The carrier vanished on its own and the
#   rule was put in place. The row read 'std/src/net DOES NOT BUILD, any new
#   test beside it fails on a foreign [P67-LEGACY] error'. Measured today:
#   `nova test std/src/net` gives PASS 4 / FAIL 0 and [P67-LEGACY] does not
#   occur at all. Ninth stale row of the day (class No.731).
#   Its rule had two halves and only the first stood: CI runs std tests
#   against a NAMED failure list. The second was missing -- that list
#   guards FAILING tests and is blind to ABSENT ones. Now measured and
#   ratcheted: 39 module dirs, 3 with no test at all (std/src/ffi,
#   std/src/unicode, std/src/runtime/string -- the string runtime).
# 2026-08-19, No.634 -- 54 -> 53. The shipped SEGV is gone: bare `${date}`
#   crashed with 0xC0000005 printing nothing, on a green check and a green
#   build. Root: the checker looked for `display` BY NAME, so seven
#   `std/time/civil` methods survived the D422 migration in the old
#   `(mut w Write)` shape while codegen called them with a `FmtCtx`.
#   Migrated, and the checker now compares the SIGNATURE.
#   The row stays OPEN by its own acceptance (enumerate every ground by
#   which a value reaches an interpolation and prove each by running), and
#   the remaining half of the class is named: a type with BOTH a
#   wrong-shaped `display` AND a `to_str` is still swallowed by the D410
#   hatch -- verified by putting the old shape back and watching the SEGV
#   return. That half closes in codegen, not in the checker.
# 2026-08-19, No.598 -- 53 -> 52. The owner's own rule is enforced at last:
#   a BORROWING method may not consume its own receiver. The promise of
#   the consume system -- given away once -- was bypassed by moving the
#   consuming call INSIDE a borrowing method, and the carrier printed
#   `first=7 second=7 third=7`: one value handed out THREE times with no
#   word from the compiler.
#   Linearity is tracked by binding NAMES and `@` has no name, so the
#   receiver's consumption entered no state at all -- the rule was written
#   down and never enforced (class O-2). Now checked by the SHAPE of the
#   call in `consume_walk_expr`.
#   Blast radius measured at ZERO: no hits across std, examples, novac or
#   the whole conformance corpus; conformance 820/0, lint 1461/0, crate
#   1250/0. Its sibling #572 was probed the same hour and is ALIVE.
# 2026-08-19, No.674 -- 52 -> 51. TWELFTH stale row of the day, and the one
#   that proves the guard's stated boundary rather than its failure.
#   Both rules are enforced, verified by RUNNING: `_y` used after an
#   underscore binding gives [E_UNDERSCORE_NAME_USED], and `Ok(_)` over a
#   linear payload gives [E_LINEAR_PAYLOAD_DISCARDED].
#   They landed in `95c5655e8 lang(D461)`, which names 674 NEITHER in its
#   subject NOR anywhere in its body -- checked by grep. So
#   check-fixed-but-open could not have linked them by any mechanism; that
#   is the boundary No.731 wrote down on the day it was built, now met in
#   the wild. What found this row was the DISCIPLINE of running the
#   carrier first, not a scanner.
# 2026-08-19, No.710 -- 51 -> 50. The handler body written in `with`
#   position was not type-checked AT ALL, and the row is closed by two
#   fixes, not one.
#   Root isolated by a ONE-VARIABLE difference: the same handler literal
#   hoisted into a variable was caught as [E7301] on the same binary,
#   while inline in `with` position it gave PASS: 1 FAIL: 0. So the
#   checker always knew HOW to check handler bodies -- the `HandlerLit`
#   arm existed and worked -- and exactly one descent was missing:
#   `f1_expr_inner` destructured `With { body, .. }` and dropped
#   `bindings`.
#   The SECOND hole was found by not believing my own "closed": the
#   bound pass has its OWN `With` node and its OWN `HandlerLit` arm,
#   declared empty with no reason given. Control on the same binary:
#   `HashMap[UserId, int].new()` with a key lacking `hash()` OUTSIDE a
#   handler -> `does not satisfy `Hash` bound`; the same call INSIDE the
#   body -> silence. Descending into `bindings` alone was NOT enough
#   there: it reaches the literal node, which then returned silently.
#   The first attempt at that probe was written on a user generic fn with
#   a bound and its CONTROL came out green -- the probe could not fail and
#   would have proved nothing (class F1, No.645). The working shape was
#   taken from a LIVE failure, examples/real_world/orm_demo.nv.
#   Radius measured BEFORE landing, as the row itself demanded; 83 files
#   carry handler literals and none is rejected.
#   NOT closed by this row, stated plainly: the typeref walker still
#   destructures `With { body, .. }`. Its probe could not fail either --
#   the control outside a handler was also silent -- so no observable hole
#   can be named there and no edit is made without a measurement.
# 2026-08-19, No.441 -- 50 -> 49. The checker never compared the DECLARED
#   failure payload with the THROWN one -- not weakly, not at all.
#   Control first: `Fail[CommitErr]` throwing `WorkErr` gave PASS: 1
#   FAIL: 0, and so did the same function throwing `CommitErr`. Nothing
#   was comparing them.
#   The spec already carried the rule (handler chosen BY VARIANT TYPE;
#   `Fail[E']` compatible when E is a subset of E'), so this enforces
#   what was written and needed no D-amendment.
#   Radius measured BEFORE any compiler code, with a static estimate over
#   the .nv sources -- and its history matters more than its result: 64
#   suspects, then 36, then 16, every cut being a fix to MY OWN estimator
#   (it did not resolve a variant to its sum; then it kept one owner per
#   variant name while `UnexpectedChar` belongs to two). The residue was
#   estimator gaps and constructor calls the check does not judge. NOT ONE
#   real mismatch exists in the tree -- which is what made the check
#   landable at all.
#   Narrow by construction: only a syntactically visible thrown type (a
#   record literal) is judged; `throw e` and `throw V(...)` are not, and a
#   variant of the declared sum is legal.
#   check-bare-type-lookups (the No.705 ratchet) caught my own patch at 20
#   against a baseline of 16. Fixed on the merits rather than by raising
#   the baseline: both name resolutions moved to `types_get_here`, and the
#   whole-table scan was dropped together with the part of the check it
#   served. Back to 16.
# 2026-08-19, No.740 -- 49 -> 50. GROWTH, and it is measurement honesty rather
#   than a weakened rule: a NEW blocker was found, not an old one re-labelled.
#   `lint-no-silent-int-fallback` reports Cat A1 at 21 against a baseline of 7 --
#   fourteen fresh sites where `type_ref_to_c` fails and a fallback type is
#   emitted SILENTLY, i.e. wrong C with no diagnostic. The sites date from
#   2026-06-11, 2026-07-24 and 2026-08-06: two months of drift.
#   Nothing runs that guard -- 0 in gate.sh, 0 in every CI workflow, 0 in any
#   script. The count did not rise because the tree got worse today; it rose
#   because someone finally looked. Hiding that by leaving the ratchet at 49
#   would be the exact dishonesty these baselines exist to prevent.
# 2026-08-21, No.740 -- 50 -> 49. NOT a closure: the ratchet was set one too
#   high on the day it was written. Measured with the guard's own core against
#   the registry AS OF THAT COMMIT -- `registry-routes-scan.py` printed
#   blockers=49 there, and prints 49 today; the 50 was a hand count that no
#   scanner ever produced. A baseline nobody re-measures is a number that
#   drifts from the thing it guards, which is the failure this file exists to
#   record rather than repeat.
#   Same session found the mirror of it in the handoff prompt: its hand-rolled
#   recipe for the same count answered 47, missing a verdict written in bold
#   (No.712, No.713) and reading "POCHINENO" as open (No.694). The recipe was
#   replaced by a pointer to this core -- one home for one number.
# 2026-08-21, No.743 -- 49 -> 50. GROWTH, and honest: a new blocker, found
#   hours after this same file was lowered 50 -> 49 for a count that was
#   never measured. Multi-argument `println` has no printability gate on
#   its arguments. Measured in ONE built and RUN program: with
#   `#impl(Debug)` on the record, `println("interp = ${p:?}")` prints
#   `Point { x: 1, y: 2 }` while `println("multiarg = ", p)` prints
#   2017309056992 -- the raw pointer as an integer, no diagnostic, exit 0.
#   The same hole with a struct-shaped value is loud instead of silent:
#   `println("at = ", rows.get(1))` passes check and dies in C. Both
#   halves reproduce with no newtype in the file, which is what the probe
#   was actually about.
# 2026-08-21, No.743 withdrawn -- 50 -> 49. The growth an hour earlier was
#   MINE and it was a DUPLICATE: No.670 has owned this class since
#   2026-08-15 (print does not ask whether the type has a representation),
#   already carries the tag verdict and already routes to window D1 with
#   No.584 and No.634. I filed a second row because I searched the
#   registry for the C-level symptom (`NovaOpt_nova_int`) and not for the
#   behaviour (a pointer printed as a number), which is how a duplicate
#   gets past a registry with 699 rows.
#   The measurement itself was NOT wasted and is folded into No.670: the
#   silent half is alive in the MULTIARG form on a type that HAS a derived
#   Debug, while interpolation of the same value in the same program is
#   correct. No.670's 2026-08-19 recheck concluded "the silence is gone"
#   after re-running only the interpolation form -- so the duplicate row
#   was wrong, and the recheck it contradicted was incomplete.
# 2026-08-22, No.744 -- 49 -> 50. GROWTH by discovery. The right operand of
#   `==` on a sum is evaluated TWICE when the variant matches: measured in a
#   built and RUN program with a call counter -- 2 evaluations on a match, 1
#   on a mismatch (the second clause is short-circuited away). Equality is
#   emitted as a chain of && clauses and the right expression is substituted
#   into each one instead of being materialised once, so a side effect there
#   happens twice. `if next_token() == Tok.Eof` eats two tokens.
#   Found by the recon window p17214-sum-stack and RE-RUN by the integrator
#   before filing -- the window's first version of this finding ("equality
#   compares tags only") did not reproduce and was withdrawn by the window
#   itself; what stayed is what a run printed.
